<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#slavery">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Slavery</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as enslaved</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Slavery is the condition in which people are treated as property and forced to work. It existed in almost every ancient and pre-modern society, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to the Islamic world and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a> made it central to the Portuguese, Spanish, British, French and Brazilian empires, while it also shaped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kongo Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/slavery.jpg" alt="Slavery" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Josiah Wedgwood (design by William Hackwood or Henry Webber) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Official_medallion_of_the_British_Anti-Slavery_Society_(1795).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The laws addressed almost every aspect of economic and social life: wages, interest rates, medical malpractice, inheritance, adoption, marriage and divorce, <b>slavery</b>, commercial partnerships and the obligations of royal officials.</blockquote><a href="/empire/babylonian-empire">Babylonian Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Battle of Chaeronea in 338 BCE a Macedonian garrison occupied the Cadmea, and when Thebes revolted in 335 BCE Alexander the Great destroyed the city and sold its population into <b>slavery</b>, with the approval of Boeotian cities that Thebes had oppressed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/boeotian-league">Boeotian League · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Slavery</b> was the empire's defining social institution: well over a million enslaved Africans were landed in Brazil between 1800 and the effective end of the transatlantic trade under the Eusébio de Queirós Law of 1850, and the agriculture of the most productive provinces rested on their labour.</blockquote><a href="/empire/brazilian-empire">Brazilian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Britain's abolition of the slave trade (1807) and of <b>slavery</b> across most of its empire (by the Slavery Abolition Act 1833), achieved only after more than a century and a half of heavy participation in the trade, was followed by naval and diplomatic pressure on other states that…</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Slavery</b> was abolished on 1 December 1834, followed by an apprenticeship that ended in 1838; compensation was paid in London and was widely felt by slave owners to be inadequate.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cape-colony">Cape Colony · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>According to Appian, many defenders killed themselves rather than surrender, the survivors were sold into <b>slavery</b> except fifty kept for Scipio Aemilianus' triumph, and the town was razed and its land divided among its neighbours.</blockquote><a href="/empire/celtiberians">Celtiberians · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
